# .release-removals — files that were in a previous release and are deliberately
# not in this one.
#
# One path or path-prefix per line; `#` comments allowed. A comment-only or blank
# file declares nothing — it is not an allow-all.
#
# If you followed a link here, you are in the right place: every entry below says
# what went away, where the thing it carried lives now, and why the name did not
# stay behind as an alias. A removal is not forbidden; an unrecorded one is.

# ── One path matcher, not two: the §4.6 type-pattern glob is gone ─────────────
# EXTENSION-TYPE §4.6 [v1.2] pins `type_pattern` to ENTITY-CORE-PROTOCOL §5.4
# `matches_pattern`. This file was a second, independent path-glob with its own
# reading of `*` and `**`, and the two answered §4.6's OWN worked example —
# `system/capability/*` against `system/capability/path-scope/foo` — with opposite
# verdicts. A `type_pattern` verdict is a validation outcome, so that divergence
# shows up as one peer accepting an entity another peer rejects.
#
# It was deleted rather than rewritten, deliberately: a second matcher that happens
# to agree today is the thing that diverges later without anyone noticing. So
# `type_pattern` now delegates to the one §5.4 `matches_pattern` in the workspace,
# and the agreement with the Go implementation was verified by enumerating both of
# its call sites rather than by finding one.
#
# What a reader loses: nothing. The behaviour did not go away; it moved to the
# single implementation the spec names, and a matcher that can disagree with it no
# longer exists in this tree to be reached by mistake.
extensions/type-system/src/glob.rs

# ── The rolling status log moved up one directory ─────────────────────────────
# `docs/status/STATUS.md` was published in the last release. It is not withdrawn:
# it MOVED, and the same content is published as **`docs/STATUS.md`**.
#
# Why. `docs/status/` holds working notes — handoffs, checkpoints, dated snapshots —
# and none of that is written for a reader outside this project. The one rolling
# status log is, because it is how the repo says out loud what it is working on and
# what it is blocked by. Separating the two by directory means the distinction holds
# by itself instead of depending on everyone remembering which single filename in
# that directory was the exception.
#
# What a reader loses: nothing. Same file, same content, one directory up.
docs/status/STATUS.md

# ── The conformance corpus is de-versioned: `vectors-v1.cbor` is superseded ────
# `conformance/vectors-v1.cbor` (69 vectors, sha256 `9d96f007…`) has been published
# since v0.8.0. It is removed, and the file that replaces it is
# **`conformance/conformance-vectors.cbor`** (71 vectors, sha256 `9695b1f1…`).
#
# They are not one corpus under two names. `vectors-v1.cbor` was an early starter
# set vendored from the Go implementation's own vectors, predating the cross-blessed
# corpus. `conformance-vectors.cbor` is the canonical corpus vendored from the
# protocol repository, which is what `conformance/MANIFEST.md` had already named as
# the source to re-vendor from. MANIFEST.md carries **both** digests — the current
# one under *Current*, this one under *Superseded* — so which corpus you are holding
# is answerable from the published tree itself, not only from this note.
#
# Why the old name did not stay as an alias. The corpus was de-versioned upstream,
# and identity here is the **digest**, not the filename. A checker keyed to the old
# filename does not go red when the name moves — it quietly becomes unable to look,
# which is not the same as a failure and does not read like one. Leaving
# `vectors-v1.cbor` on disk as a courtesy is exactly what lets such a checker keep
# passing against a corpus nobody re-vendors.
#
# What a reader loses: a path. Anything pinning `conformance/vectors-v1.cbor` gets a
# 404 and should move to `conformance/conformance-vectors.cbor` — and should verify
# the digest in MANIFEST.md rather than the name. This is called a breaking change in
# the changelog entry for this release; it is declared, not smuggled.
conformance/vectors-v1.cbor
